Chapter 10, Section 8: "The Result of Choice"

"I cannot thank you enough for your continued concern for this household."

"No, it's only natural. It's not something you need to thank me for."

Saying this with great humility, Subaru waved both hands—which rested on Beatrice's lap—flusteredly at the person serving them tea, Wilhelm, in response to his words.

Beatrice cast a sidelong, exasperated glare at Subaru's gesture, but she likely understood his nervousness from the heartbeat she could feel against his back. She sighed, seemingly resigned, and continued to devote herself to being Subaru's puppet.

Having just finished their meeting with Philoyre, who had thrown the Royal Selection into chaos, Subaru and the others, upon hearing the news at the Dragon Faith Church that Crusch had awakened, found themselves unable to stand still and had rushed straight to the Karsten residence in the noble district like this.

Of course, following proper etiquette, making an appointment beforehand was essential. It was a visit so socially inept they'd even forgotten that, yet they were kindly welcomed in.

Ushered into the reception room by Wilhelm, the four of them (with Beatrice on Subaru's lap) sat side-by-side on the sofa, eagerly awaiting that very moment.

Perhaps noticing Subaru's impatience, Wilhelm bowed deeply.

"Please allow a little more time for Lady Crusch to prepare. My apologies."

"No, no, we're the ones who barged in unannounced. Honestly, we should be grateful we weren't turned away."

"Turned away? Lady Crusch herself was overjoyed that you, Subaru-dono, and Emilia-sama came to visit."

"No, no, no, about that, it might actually be the opposite—or maybe it will be—I might just burst into tears when I see Lady Crusch awake, so..."

It was a pitiful admission, but there was a real possibility it could happen, so he wanted to give Wilhelm, Emilia, and the others a heads-up beforehand.

For now, just trying to contain his excitement, Subaru responded to Wilhelm's remarks by waving Beatrice's arms vigorously back and forth, accompanying each "No."

"Hey, you're swinging Betty's hands around way too much! My shoulders and arms are starting to get tired, you know!"

"Ugh... Sorry, my bad, I just can't calm down. Because..."

Beatrice, who had been diligently serving as Subaru's emotional support puppet, reached her limit and barked at him. Poking Beatrice's cheek, Subaru paused for a moment.

He was impatient and anxious. But it was worth the wait.

"Because Lady Crusch is..."

"Yes... That is certainly true."

At Subaru's sigh, laden with myriad emotions, Wilhelm nodded just as deeply, moved. —No, to claim it was the same was the height of presumption.

Wilhelm's relief was surely many, many times greater than Subaru's. The recovery of the one he had sworn to serve was an event of that magnitude.

"I've said it many times already, but Wilhelm-san, I'm truly glad for you," said Emilia, sitting next to Subaru, once again expressing her appreciation for Wilhelm.

Emilia, who had been present when Philoyre used the Church's sacrament to treat Crusch, had already offered her condolences to Wilhelm and the others that same day. However, since Crusch hadn't regained consciousness after that, even Emilia's violet-blue eyes sparkled with anticipation at the prospect of finally being able to speak with the awakened Crusch.

"Oh dear, at this rate, I'm worried about these two making a blunder. Rem, it might be up to Betty and us to keep things in order, or things could get out of hand."

"Indeed. Please leave it to us if they go too far. I've been gradually getting used to handling and feeling the weight of the iron ball."

With Subaru and Emilia being so restless, having Beatrice and Rem maintain their composure as visitors was reassuring, though the faint clinking of Rem's chain whenever she moved was both scary and exciting. Asking Rem for backup seemed like it would require considerable resolve from Subaru.

"Oh, speaking of backup, where's Ferris? Is he helping Lady Crusch change? There are things I want to talk to him about too."

Suddenly noticing a face that should be present was missing, Subaru voiced his question.

Subaru and the others, and Wilhelm, would naturally be overjoyed at Crusch's recovery. But surely the one feeling it most keenly was none other than Crusch's foremost knight.

Of course, the fact that Crusch's salvation came through the Dragon Faith Church must have been deeply painful for him as a healer, but Subaru had no doubt he was the kind of person who would rejoice in Crusch's safety over succumbing to self-recrimination from powerlessness.

However—

"—"

"—? Wilhelm-san?"

Noticing Wilhelm's cheek momentarily tense, Subaru frowned in puzzlement. But Wilhelm quickly erased the tension with an "I apologize."

"Regarding Ferris... Actually, he is feeling slightly unwell. It seems the stress and strain of recent events all came rushing out at once after Lady Crusch's condition was addressed."

"Oh no, that happened afterward? ...I'm worried about Ferris. Even though Lady Crusch finally woke up, Ferris will blame himself for collapsing."

"Yeah, right. Wilhelm-san, is Ferris in bad shape?"

"...No, rest should cure him. However, I would ask that you refrain from visiting him for now. I believe he himself would not wish to disturb the feelings of those who have come to see Lady Crusch."

"We wouldn't think of it as disturbing... but yes, I understand. We'll do it another time."

Emilia nodded at Wilhelm's suggestion, acknowledging the circumstances, and Subaru agreed. They planned to stay in the capital for a while longer. Even if they missed today, there would be other opportunities to thank Ferris. For now, it was best to respect his wishes. —Just as Subaru and the others were thinking this.

"I am so sorry to have kept you waiting."

At the sight of the person who opened the reception room door saying this, Subaru involuntarily stood up.

There at the entrance stood a beautiful woman—Crusch Karsten herself—with her characteristic long green hair and slender frame, wearing dark sleepwear draped with a pink stole.

"—"

When he last saw her, Crusch had been struggling to breathe, in such a state that simply sitting up in bed was difficult. Seeing her standing on her own two feet and appearing before them like this filled him with immense joy and emotion.

However, as she looked at Subaru with a faint, ephemeral smile, half of her face remained wrapped in bandages covering her left eye, and that inevitably drew his attention first...

"Please don't worry. These bandages are only because I still have some trouble seeing from one eye. Most of the affliction... no, the affliction itself has been removed."

"Ah..."

"So please, don't look so troubled."

Chided gently, Subaru realized he had come to offer comfort but instead ended up making her worry about him. While he was surprised that the bandages covering her face remained, the fact that Crusch had come to see them on her own two feet was far more significant. Her illness had been so severe that simply standing would have seemed an unimaginable feat.

"—"

"—Subaru-sama?"

Noticing Subaru had fallen silent, Crusch, who had been smiling, narrowed her amber eyes. Meeting that gaze—now only one—Subaru carefully chose his words. After all, if he misspoke even slightly, his composure might crumble into droplets.

"...You know, Lady Crusch, I had so many things I wanted to say. Mostly things like 'I'm sorry,' 'my bad'—just words of apology."

"—. Yes."

"But meeting you face-to-face like this, all that comes out now is how glad I am, how relieved I am. I'm truly, truly relieved..."

He was sure he had prepared several model responses befitting a visitor beforehand. On the way to the mansion, during this waiting time, he had frantically simulated the perfect get-well message in his head, convinced he had selected the best possible words.

Yet all of them vanished the instant he met the real Crusch, leaving only this childish, immature, and formless sense of relief.

"—"

Hearing Subaru's pathetic self-confession, Crusch's eyes widened for a moment before a soft smile returned to her lips.

"Having you say that, I am also relieved. I have shown you my disgraceful state so often, I thought you might have grown tired of me by now."

"That's not true! Not at all, absolutely not! More importantly, we shouldn't make you stand and talk while you're still recovering. Please, have a seat."

"Hehe, then I shall accept your kind offer."

Flustered and thrown off balance, Subaru offered her a chair opposite them. Crusch, laughing with a hand over her mouth, accepted his escort. Subaru had somehow taken the lead, but since it was her house, being laughed at was only natural.

Feeling awkward in the conversation, Subaru watched as Wilhelm helped Crusch sit, then shot a resentful look at Emilia and the others nearby.

"Hey, isn't it unfair that I'm the only one running around in circles? Why am I the one being played?"

"Well, you were the most worried about Crusch-san, weren't you? So I thought you'd want to talk to her first," Emilia replied.

"I am not in a position to intrude here. Besides, there was also the matter with Beatrice-chan," Rem added.

"The matter with Beako... Huh? Why is Beako on Rem's lap? Weren't you sitting on my lap?"

"I was, but you flung me off when you stood up! If Rem hadn't caught me so skillfully, I'd be sprawled out in the middle of the room by now!"

"O-Oh, right, sorry about that. You were literally out of my sight..."

"That's a terrible way to put it!"

Beatrice puffed out her cheeks and clung stubbornly to Rem. Leaving her in Rem's embrace, Subaru reflected that he really had been oblivious to his surroundings.

Crusch deepened her smile at their exchange, letting out another soft "Fufu." Seeing Crusch's reaction, Emilia narrowed her violet-blue eyes.

"I'm sorry we're all so noisy even though we came to visit you."

"No, I don't dislike liveliness. Besides... I am also happy that you came to see me immediately upon hearing the news."

"That's good then. Ah, but I'll make sure they don't get too carried away."

Concerned for Crusch's condition, Emilia added a final remark characteristic of her. It showed consideration and an intention to choose her words carefully, reflecting Emilia's visible growth, even in a situation like this.

Of course, taking Crusch's words entirely at face value might not be wise either...

"How are you feeling physically?"

"Yes, much better. It will likely take some time to regain my strength, but there don't seem to be any major problems."

Crusch answered, adjusting the stole on her shoulder. Still recovering, her complexion was somewhat pale, but her responses were natural, and her usual intelligence remained undimmed. From what he could see, her gait was steady, and aside from the bandage covering her left eye, no traces of that day seemed to remain. For now, he could breathe a sigh of relief.

However—

"I have also received reports about what transpired while I was bedridden... Including the news that Priscilla-sama has passed away."

If her intelligence remained intact, Crusch fully understood that they couldn't simply rejoice in her physical recovery without acknowledging the circumstances.

"—"

Even knowing it was an unavoidable topic, it seemed too soon to bring it up. Hearing it brought up by Crusch herself made Subaru and the others stiffen. They had hoped to celebrate her recovery first and save deeper discussions about the Royal Selection for another time—but she clearly wanted none of such empty reassurances.

"As far as I can recall, I hardly exchanged any words with Priscilla-sama. However, there is no doubt she participated in the Battle of Priestella... and by extension, the Royal Selection, with her own sense of honor and conviction. That is precisely why we must continue the Royal Selection, for her sake as well."

"—. Yes, I feel the same way. I believe we mustn't be disheartened forever, for Priscilla's sake too."

"I see. I'm glad. Emilia-sama shares the same view."

Sighing with relief in front of a nodding Emilia, Crusch brought her teacup to her lips. Moistening them, she fixed her gaze upon them with her single amber eye, and then said, "In that case."

"Then I believe you will agree that the most important thing now is the stability of the kingdom. While the Witch Cult's actions were indeed a regrettable incident in the Royal Selection, devoting too much time to countermeasures against them would deviate from the original purpose of the Royal Selection itself."

"Hmm, I understand that too. The Royal Selection is meant to decide the kingdom's future, not to deal with the Witch Cult. Is that what you mean?"

"Yes, precisely. Of course, dealing with them is a challenge any ruler must face, but prioritizing that would be playing right into their hands. We must remember the true purpose of the Royal Selection itself."

"Then Felt-chan is also in the capital right now. Anastasia-san doesn't seem to be able to return yet, but if we can discuss things, I'd be happy to—"

"—Furthermore," Crusch interrupted Emilia mid-sentence, leaning forward slightly. For an instant, it felt like being confronted with a drawn blade, and Emilia's eyes widened slightly.

Ignoring Emilia's reaction, Crusch intensified the gleam in her amber eye.

"I heard that you have opened the path to the Sage's Tower... the Pleiades Watchtower. I am impressed by such a remarkable achievement. If that tower holds the power rumored, it should serve as sufficient leverage against other countries. Its handling must be carefully considered with the Council of Wise Men."

"Crusch-san..."

"The deadline for the crucial Dragon Sacred Ceremony... the voting day to decide the next king, is now less than a year and a half away. From now on, the validity of each camp's claims will be scrutinized more than ever. We must look more closely at the people around us and listen more carefully to their voices than before."

"Wait, Crusch-san, I want to talk. But don't rush so much. Right now—"

"—I!"

"—!"

Just as Emilia tried to stem the torrent of words pouring from Crusch with emotion, a powerful shout sliced through the reception room.

Crusch's teacup was roughly returned to its saucer with a sharp clink, spilling tea. Instantly, Wilhelm pulled Crusch's hand away, dabbed her hand with a handkerchief, and moved the cup aside.

Throughout, Crusch, motionless, never once took her eyes off Subaru and the others.

Without looking away, her thin lips trembling, she asked,

"Am I... in the wrong...?"

"—"

Her previously resolute, intelligent tone wavered, the question wet with tears. No tears actually welled in Crusch's eyes. But her voice was crying. The vast disparity between her reason and emotion cut deeply into Subaru's chest.

Crusch understood. She was intelligent, possessed excellent insight, and was a hard worker who had continually faced responsibilities and challenges. Regardless of whether Subaru and the others chose their words carefully or not, it didn't matter.

She knew that the manner of her salvation was unworthy of the king she aspired to be.

"...There's no way that's true."

Reflecting on the conviction blooming within himself, Subaru ground his teeth. He had to say it was ridiculous, utterly absurd. To say the manner of her salvation was wrong—that would be like saying it would have been better for Crusch to still be suffering from the Witch Cult's 'Lust.' That was nonsense. Why should someone as admirable as Crusch have her dreams and goals thwarted by the actions of ridiculous people like the Witch Cult?

It was an unreasonable suffering. An irrationality she had no reason to endure. So what if she was saved by someone's kindness? 'Thank you for saving me. I won't forget this gratitude, and I'll continue to do my best.' —What was wrong with that?

"—You're not wrong at all."

Within his heart surged a feeling accompanied by an irrepressible anger. Pushed by it, before he knew it, Subaru had stood again, and he tightly grasped Crusch's hand across the low table.

Crusch's white fingers, from which the handkerchief had fallen, bore no trace of those ugly black spots. This was Crusch's hand as it should be, with no reason to be defiled by anyone.

"Subaru-sama."

"You're not wrong. Not one bit. Let's properly do the Royal Selection. Let's talk properly, think properly, and compete properly. I'll do whatever it takes."

It was a declaration made knowing he might be scolded or rebuked for making reckless promises. They were from opposing camps. Anyone would think it would be a huge advantage for Emilia if the powerful rival Crusch dropped out of the Royal Selection.

"But that's not it. That's not right. The Royal Selection we all envisioned, where each of us wants our candidate to be king, shouldn't be decided that way."

"—"

"So, Lady Crusch, you're not wrong at all."

He wasn't confident whether his words had formed a logical conclusion. However, overwhelmed by Subaru's impassioned outburst, Crusch, though slightly taken aback, let out a sigh as if some tension within her had eased.

"See, Subaru went and said everything I was going to say first. That's cheating," Emilia pouted from the sofa, chiding Subaru's outburst. Suddenly realizing he had gotten carried away alone, Subaru felt his ears burn. Thinking he should settle down, he began to return to his seat—

"—. Subaru-sama, that hand..."

"Huh? My hand?"

Pointed out, Subaru looked down at his own hand that had just been holding Crusch's. He thought he was being called out for his tremendous rudeness, but that wasn't it. What caught Crusch's amber eye wasn't Subaru's rudeness, but—

"Subaru-sama's hand. It was afflicted like my body... Could it be that Subaru-sama's hand was also healed by the power of the Dragon Faith Church?"

"Ah, no, this was more violent—it was the result of a 'boom,' you could say. I don't know the detailed method myself, so it's honestly kind of a scary story."

In fact, Subaru's hand had now returned to its original skin color, but the process was quite violent and not something anyone could imitate. To put it bluntly, it was burned off, blasted away, and regenerated. —Currently, his foot was in the same state, but he had no desire to test if burning and blasting it off would make it regenerate.

"—i"

"—"

Flexing his hand while recalling that extreme treatment, Subaru thought he heard a faint whisper and involuntarily held his breath. He blinked and looked at Crusch before him. It couldn't be, he thought.

"Crusch-san, please rest slowly for now. After that, like Subaru said earlier, I intend to do everything I can."

"...Thank you. You may come to regret this, you know?"

"I believe I now understand better than before the difference between regrets worth having and those that aren't."

However, the sense of dissonance and doubt that had crept into Subaru was pushed aside by the conversation between Emilia and Crusch over his head—a discussion befitting two Royal Selection candidates. In truth, Subaru and Emilia's proposal was nothing short of giving aid to an enemy, but winning against Crusch by avoiding that would make true joy impossible. So this proposal and declaration of intent aligned with the Emilia camp's desires.

"—Fuu."

As their exchange concluded, Crusch let out a sigh tinged with fatigue. Still recovering and lacking strength, she had also experienced an emotional moment. Physically, the visit had become quite lengthy for Crusch.

"We should be going now. Emilia-san."

"Yes. Thank you for seeing us, even though you're so terribly tired and it's a difficult time."

"No, I should be the one apologizing for not being able to offer you proper hospitality. Wilhelm, would you please see everyone out?"

Prompted by Rem, Emilia brought the visit to a close. Smiling, Crusch instructed Wilhelm beside her. Wilhelm bowed and accepted, and as Subaru and the others prepared to leave, Crusch also stood to see them off.

And then—

"Thank you so much for coming today. I am truly glad to have seen you."

"—"

Apologizing for the discourtesy of seeing them off from there, Crusch addressed Subaru and the others. For a moment, Subaru hesitated, wondering if he should say something—

"—Subaru-dono, allow me to see you out."

As if sensing his hesitation, Wilhelm interposed and cut him off.

***

"I was terribly rude earlier."

On the way from the reception room to the mansion entrance, Wilhelm, walking ahead, began. Hearing this, Subaru, holding hands with Beatrice, widened his eyes, thinking, "Just as I thought."

"I noticed you stopped me at the end. ...But honestly, I don't even know what I was about to say myself back there."

"Even so, it was impertinent of me to interrupt a guest's words. It was solely out of concern for Lady Crusch's wellbeing, but I apologize for my rudeness."

"...Wilhelm-san, you noticed too, didn't you? How Lady Crusch was."

"Of course, she is far from fully recovered and will require further rest. However, even if her body and mind heal..."

Wilhelm trailed off, looking down. But Subaru could roughly guess what he left unsaid. No one had voiced it clearly in that room. But Crusch herself felt it most keenly, which was why such poignant emotion had spilled out.

And besides—

"Ferris must be frustrated too. He must want to be by Crusch-san's side supporting her more than anyone right now, but his body won't let him," Emilia murmured worriedly, glancing back toward the reception room they had just left. At Emilia's words, Wilhelm's throat constricted slightly.

Then, without stopping, he addressed Emilia-sama in a solemn tone.

"Earlier, I told a lie."

"Huh? A lie?"

"Yes. —Concerning Ferris."

At the words and name, Subaru and the others exchanged glances. Wilhelm had mentioned a lie involving Ferris. According to his explanation, Ferris was unwell and resting, but—

"No, that's strange. Doesn't Ferris... not get sick in the first place?"

"Well, he's not like... Reinhardt-san, after all."

"Of course, Reinhardt is extraordinary, but Ferris is also one of the extraordinary ones in that sense..."

Leaving aside Reinhardt, the internationally astonishing man who could carry a dragon carriage and leap away, Ferris was a healer—holder of the 'Blue' title, reputed to be the kingdom's strongest. Subaru had once witnessed Ferris caught in a Witch Cultist's suicide bombing, only to return calmly from the scene where the dragon carriage was shattered.

He was, so to speak, the kind of super healer familiar from manga and anime. Of course, he had exhausted his mana and devoted himself entirely to Crusch, so what applied then might not necessarily apply now.

"As Subaru-dono has noticed, Ferris is not bedridden. He is not currently in this mansion."

"Not in the mansion? That means..."

"—He has gone to the Royal Mausoleum."

Without answering the unexpectedly raised question, Wilhelm tersely gave the location and led Subaru and the others to the mansion entrance. The Royal Mausoleum—just hearing it, the purpose seemed obvious.

But Wilhelm's true intent in telling them about that place was—

"No matter how much shame I must bear, I am currently one who serves Lady Crusch. I cannot act against my master's wishes. —I implore you, please, hear him out."

With a deep bow, Wilhelm saw them off. The look of anguish etched on his deeply lined face conveyed that this was a problem even the 'Sword Demon' could not solve with his sword, yet one he hoped would be resolved even if through bitter struggle—it felt like a prayer.









----



――The Royal Mausoleum was located at the farthest reaches of the noble district in the royal capital, Lugnica.

As the sound of the name and the characters it evoked suggested, this place was a cemetery—a resting place for souls prayed for peace, lined with numerous tombstones to mourn the dead.

"There was the White Whale subjugation, right? They say the graves of the people who took part in that battle and lost their lives are here too," Emilia said.

"I see. Guess I've been pretty heartless never coming here. We didn't bring any flowers today, so next time we come, we'll have to prepare properly."

"Yeah. We need to say thank you so much and well done to all of them."

Passing through the black iron gate, unmarred by any trace of rust, they found a paved path laid out in geometric patterns leading forward, flanked on both sides by rows of grave markers—not a single one of the tombstones standing there could be called a mere stone slab.

Some were crowned with statues of knights boasting of their deeds in life; others were crafted like small shrines with intricate carvings narrating the history of a family. Flags engraved with family crests fluttered in the wind, and the colorful flower bouquets offered by visitors provided the only vibrancy in this world of gray stone.

"As expected, nobles sure are show-offs even with their tombstones... But aside from the flashiness, I guess the impression is close to cemeteries I've seen in Western movies or dramas," Subaru mused.

Well-maintained lawns and the paved path spread out around a gentle hill. There were no familiar Japanese-style tombstones, stupas, or shrine/temple buildings nearby—just a cool, lonely-feeling space where the wind blew through.

"Come to think of it, I never really considered it before... but over here, are the dead cremated? Or buried?"

"Basically, cremation, I'd think. When a living being dies, the soul... the Od, leaves the body. A body without Od just slowly crumbles and decays, becoming unsightly. So before that happens, we indicate that the body, like the Od, has fulfilled its purpose," Beatrice explained.

"I see, so it's not just for disease prevention, but based on the very nature of existence."

Thanks to Beatrice's immediate answer to his question, the reasoning made sense to Subaru. No wonder during the 'Great Disaster' in Vollachia, the many zombies that rose again weren't rotted or missing limbs or in incomplete states. In those cases, their bodies were formed from earth, based on the shape of the souls used for their resurrection. So those zombies had returned to the world in their former likeness.

"That makes the place where souls end up... 'Od Lagna's Cradle' or whatever, a pretty valuable place. Like the Sanzu River, or the End of Time."

"Don't suddenly start muttering such frightening things. 'Od Lagna's Cradle' is not something you should even joke about. The rumor is that if you accidentally meet its gaze, you'll lose your sanity," Beatrice warned, grimacing.

"No, I'm not joking, I really... but that was just Louis talking on her own, so who knows how credible it is?"

Chastised by Beatrice, Subaru recalled that pure white space where he had spent such an intense time, for better or worse. Louis Arneb, posing as the gatekeeper of that place, had called it the 'Corridor of Memories,' but while it might have had that aspect, what it actually was remained unknown. Louis's consciousness had vanished somewhere, and now that she had been reborn as Spica, there was no way to verify it.

"If I had to, I could try finding Reid's book again and peek inside, prepared for something terrible to happen..."

"Um, I hate to interrupt your conversation, but can I ask something?"

"Huh?"

"You came here looking for someone, didn't you? Ferris-san, was it?"

Rem tugged at his sleeve, and as Subaru turned, she asked him this. Rem, with a look of deep puzzlement in her eyes, naturally didn't know anything about Ferris's character. Being brought to a cemetery without that knowledge must have left her feeling at a loss.

"Oh, sorry, sorry! Um, the one we're looking for is a cat-eared, kinda 'kyurun' type person. He's got a tail too, and he's pretty cute, so I think you'll know him when you see him," Subaru explained.

"...I could use a bit more helpful information," Rem replied flatly.

"I thought I gave some pretty good hints... Ah, when I say cute, I don't mean Beako-cute, more like Petra or Meili-cute. He's about as tall as me."

"I see. There is indeed a difference in cuteness," Rem nodded, seemingly convinced by Subaru's explanation, having held Beatrice up in front of her face and studied her features intently. Beatrice herself looked puzzled by that assessment, but it could be considered a charm point that she was unaware of the nuances of her own cuteness.

Indeed, cuteness comes in many forms—the cute side of pretty, the pretty side of cute, and so on. However, even having shared Ferris's characteristics—

"I wonder what Wilhelm-san's attitude meant. I'm worried," Emilia said.

"Him knowing where Ferris is and wanting us to go talk to him... that's something, too."

"Yeah, right. It's nice being consulted or relied on, but I feel like Crusch-san and Wilhelm-san don't usually show us that side of themselves," Emilia added.

Subaru could agree with Emilia's feeling.

What he could gather was that some sort of problem had arisen within the Crusch camp, and Wilhelm himself couldn't act to solve it. Of course, Subaru could think of a likely trigger for that problem. But even with that, the Crusch camp's bond was strong. Those two were bound by the longest and strongest ties compared to any of the other Royal Selection candidates. The depth of that feeling hadn't disappeared even when Crusch lost her memories—at least, that's what Subaru had felt and believed.

However—

"—"

Staring intently at his own right hand, Subaru glared at his lifeline. What flashed through his mind was the moment he had rudely grasped Crusch's hand and she had looked back at his. Seeing his hand freed from those black markings, and hearing that it wasn't through the Dragon Faith Church's sacrament, she had—

"—So unfair."

—Whispered those words in a voice so faint it seemed about to vanish.

"It wasn't a mishearing, was it..."

He desperately wished it was, but he didn't possess ears or an ego that convenient. That was undoubtedly Crusch's dark, true feelings leaking from her lips. Honestly, it was shocking. But to wish that Crusch wouldn't even utter such things would be pure arrogance. Above all, Subaru understood painfully well why Crusch had let those words slip.

"...There's no way I can bring that up with Crusch-san."

Crusch, at the center of it all, having just recovered physically, now faced the possibility of bearing countless wounds on her heart. He couldn't directly question her. Could he perhaps ask Ferris, her knight, instead? That wasn't certain either. But he knew that if he returned to his companions without doing anything, he'd just continue to agonize over this unanswered question forever.

"Hmm, I can't find Ferris anywhere," Emilia said.

Lost in thought, Subaru realized they had already made a full circle of the cemetery. The grounds weren't small, but as a cemetery in the noble district, most of the dead resting here were likely those who had borne heavy responsibilities for the kingdom, and their posthumous treatment was appropriately respectful. The deeper they went, the larger and more elaborate the grave markers became, and the fewer visitors there were, making faces easier to spot, so it was unlikely they had overlooked Ferris. If only they knew who he had come to visit, it might be different.

"We've looked around, but I don't see any woman matching the description," Rem reported.

"Strictly speaking, he's not a girl... no, that doesn't matter for appearance, so never mind."

"—? The only other prominent place would be that building, wouldn't it?"

Puzzled by Subaru's vague explanation, Rem pointed toward a white stone building seated on the central hill—a mausoleum, so to speak, with the orderly rows of stone monuments behind it. Its solemn and sacred aura, with the surrounding grave markers arranged as if swearing fealty to their master, testified that this was the resting place of the chosen.

If this was the Royal Mausoleum, and within it lay the resting place of the specially honored dead—

"—That looks like the mausoleum where the royal family rests," Subaru said.

"So the deceased royalty are inside that..."

The trigger for the Royal Selection's beginning, the ones who had carried on the proud bloodline of the long-standing Lugnica Kingdom up to the present, meeting their abrupt end—the royalty. Facing the mausoleum where they were interred, Subaru finally understood, albeit belatedly. —The solemn atmosphere pervading this place was, in a sense, a microcosm of the royal capital. If the royal castle was the symbol of authority for the living, then the mausoleum was the same for the dead—

"...I guess we can't just casually visit the kings' graves, can we?"

"Normally, a royal mausoleum would be under heavy guard, I'd think. There are plenty of people who would try to misuse the ashes of the powerful for wicked deeds. Disgraceful, isn't it?" Beatrice said.

"But we could at least ask who's inside... Oh, perfect timing."

Although the mausoleum was the only place they hadn't checked, it wasn't a place they could easily enter. The possibility of Ferris being there was low, but having been entrusted with this by Wilhelm, he didn't want to return with the job half done.

Just as he was thinking this, Emilia pointed sharply at the mausoleum entrance. Sure enough, a figure had just emerged from inside.

A slender person, dressed in something like a dark navy tailcoat. The grace of their movement, with their back turned, strongly conveyed respect and reverence for the mausoleum. Since they had been entering and exiting the royal mausoleum, Subaru assumed they might be the caretaker or someone similar, and hurried after Emilia, who had already dashed ahead.

"Um, excuse me? We were wondering about the building you just came out of—could you tell us if the person we're looking for is inside?" Emilia called out to the figure without hesitation.

Her boldness might have invited caution, but guards stood at the cemetery entrance and had checked their identities when they passed through the gate, so no suspicious persons were supposed to be inside. Subaru hoped the figure would understand that intention—

"—Emilia-sama?"

The figure in the tailcoat turned at the surprised voice and looked at Emilia. Seeing them from the front, Emilia stopped in her tracks with a surprised "Eh?" And then Subaru and the others, looking past the halted Emilia, also saw them.

Standing there was—

"...Well, well. Caught me in a strange place, didn't you? It was Wilhelm, right?" Ferris said, shrugging at them with a faint, wry smile that mixed exasperation and self-mockery, his slender frame clearly clad in a men's tailcoat.

"—"

Seeing Ferris with the mausoleum entrance behind him, Subaru was struck speechless. Ferris had worn such attire before—indeed, he had donned the uniform of the Knight Guard, dressed in men's clothing. But that had been under the guise of the official Knights' uniform, and even then, Subaru thought he had put his own stylish spin on it.

But the Ferris before them, in that tailcoat, had none of that flair or pretense. The meaning of it was—

"...Ferris, what happened?" Subaru asked.

"Don't make such a face and voice like it's the end of the world. It's nothing major for you guys, Subaru-kun. It's just—" Ferris trailed off, tilting his head slightly with that same wry smile—then stopped. As if that gesture itself signified a farewell to such mannerisms.

True to that impression, Ferris continued.

"—It's just, I was dismissed as Lady Crusch's knight. So Ferri-chan is done for, and I've gone back to being Felix Argyle."







